Write a self-contained HTML lesson file to this exact path:
/home/node/work/normal_work/HFT_Trading_Practice_Repo/docs/learning/lessons/0004-itch-order-book-latency.html

Use the dark-theme style from the sibling files (same CSS variables: --bg:#0f1117, --surface:#1a1d27, --surface2:#22263a, --border:#2e3450, --accent:#5b8dee, --accent2:#a78bfa, --green:#34d399, --yellow:#fbbf24, --red:#f87171, --text:#e2e8f0, --muted:#94a3b8, --code-bg:#0d1117, --inline-code-bg:#1e2436). Inline all CSS. max-width 860px container. Dark code blocks. Same header structure: small lesson-id label "LESSON 0004", then h1, subtitle.

Title: "Limit Order Book Reconstruction from a Binary Message Protocol (ITCH 5.0)"

Cover all 7 concepts thoroughly, in order, with real code snippets from the actual source files (shown below). Each concept should be a section with an h2.

Concept 1 — ITCH 5.0 binary format
- MoldUDP64 framing: 2-byte big-endian length prefix then payload. The parser reads `be16(buf+pos)` to get msg_len, then advances pos by 2+msg_len.
- Message type byte is the first byte of the payload. Enum MsgType: AddOrder='A', ExecuteOrder='E', DeleteOrder='D', ReplaceOrder='U'.
- All multi-byte fields are big-endian. Show the be16/be32/be64 helpers from itch_parser.cpp:
```cpp
static inline uint16_t be16(const uint8_t* p) {
    return static_cast<uint16_t>(p[0] << 8 | p[1]);
}
static inline uint32_t be32(const uint8_t* p) {
    return (static_cast<uint32_t>(p[0]) << 24 |
            static_cast<uint32_t>(p[1]) << 16 |
            static_cast<uint32_t>(p[2]) <<  8 |
            static_cast<uint32_t>(p[3]));
}
```
- Add Order field layout (36 bytes): [0] type 1B, [1] stock locate 2B, [3] tracking 2B, [5] timestamp 6B, [11] order_ref 8B, [19] side 1B, [20] shares 4B, [24] stock 8B, [32] price 4B
- Price is stored as a fixed-point integer: price_raw / 10000.0 = real price. So $150.25 is stored as 1502500.

Concept 2 — Order reference number as the primary key
- Every live order has a unique uint64_t order_ref. Add creates it, Execute/Delete/Replace reference it.
- The four operations:
  - Add: inserts a new order_ref into the hash map
  - Execute: reduces shares at the order_ref (may fully remove it)
  - Delete: removes the order_ref entirely
  - Replace: deletes old_order_ref and adds new_order_ref with new price/shares
- Show the OrderRecord struct and orders_ map from order_book.hpp:
```cpp
struct OrderRecord {
    char     side;
    uint32_t price_raw;
    uint32_t shares;
};
// orders_[ref] → OrderRecord (side, price, shares)
std::unordered_map<uint64_t, OrderRecord> orders_;
```
- Why the hash map exists: given any Execute/Delete/Replace, we need O(1) lookup of side+price to update the correct level map.

Concept 3 — L2 book reconstruction
- Two separate std::map containers, one for bids, one for asks. Key = price_raw, value = total shares.
- Bids sorted descending (std::greater): bids_.begin() is the best bid (highest price).
- Asks sorted ascending (default): asks_.begin() is the best ask (lowest price).
- Show the declarations from order_book.hpp:
```cpp
std::map<uint32_t, uint32_t, std::greater<uint32_t>> bids_;
std::map<uint32_t, uint32_t> asks_;
```
- TopOfBook struct: optional<PriceLevel> best_bid, optional<PriceLevel> best_ask. Returns nullopt when that side is empty.
- Explain that std::map gives O(log n) per operation but best bid/ask is always O(1) via .begin() — no scanning required.

Concept 4 — Top-of-book signal
- After every book update, make_signal(book) is called and emits a TopOfBookSignal.
- Show the TopOfBookSignal struct and make_signal template from signal.hpp:
```cpp
struct TopOfBookSignal {
    double bid_price;   // best bid in real price units (raw / 10000.0)
    double ask_price;   // best ask in real price units
    double mid;         // (bid + ask) / 2.0; NaN if one side is empty
    uint32_t bid_shares;
    uint32_t ask_shares;
    bool valid;         // false if either side is empty
};

template<typename Book>
inline TopOfBookSignal make_signal(const Book& book) {
    TopOfBookSignal sig{};
    const TopOfBook tob = book.top_of_book();
    if (tob.best_bid && tob.best_ask) {
        sig.bid_price  = tob.best_bid->price_raw / 10000.0;
        sig.ask_price  = tob.best_ask->price_raw / 10000.0;
        sig.mid        = (sig.bid_price + sig.ask_price) / 2.0;
        sig.valid      = true;
    }
    return sig;
}
```
- The mid-price as the simplest alpha signal: it moves when either the best bid or best ask changes.
- valid=false means one or both sides of the book are empty — signal should be suppressed downstream.

Concept 5 — Tick-to-trade latency
- Definition: time from the first byte of a message arriving to the signal being emitted.
- In bench_main.cpp, t0 is captured immediately before book.add() and t1 immediately after make_signal(book). The delta is the tick-to-trade latency for that message.
- Show the pattern from bench_main.cpp:
```cpp
visitor.on_add = [&](const itch::AddOrder& m) {
    const uint64_t t0 = now_ns();
    book.add(m.order_ref, m.side, m.price, m.shares);
    itch::make_signal(book);
    const uint64_t t1 = now_ns();
    hist.record(t1 - t0);
};
```
- Note: the parse step itself is NOT included in t0–t1 — the message has already been parsed by the time on_add fires. This is intentional: the parse latency is separate from the book-update+signal latency.
- In a real colocation setup, the reference point would be the NIC timestamp when the first byte of the UDP packet arrives (hardware timestamping).

Concept 6 — HdrHistogram percentiles
- Why not just average latency? The average hides outliers. In HFT, a single 10ms spike can blow a position.
- p50 (median): 50% of messages processed in ≤ this value. The "typical" case.
- p99: 99% of messages processed within this. The "tail you must budget for" in your strategy.
- p99.9: 1-in-1000 events. Catches GC pauses, OS scheduling jitter, cache misses.
- In bench_main.cpp output: `printf("Latency (ns): p50=%llu p99=%llu p99.9=%llu mean=%.1f max=%llu\n", ...)`
- The latency::Histogram uses power-of-2 buckets with sub-bucket resolution (HDR = High Dynamic Range). It covers 1ns–68s at ~1% precision without allocating per-sample.
- Show the record and percentile API from latency_histogram.hpp:
```cpp
void record(uint64_t value);          // record one latency sample in ns
uint64_t percentile(double pct) const; // pct in [0.0, 100.0]
```

Concept 7 — CLOCK_MONOTONIC vs wall clock
- CLOCK_REALTIME tracks wall-clock time and can jump backward (NTP adjustments, leap seconds). If a negative latency is recorded, your benchmark is broken.
- CLOCK_MONOTONIC guarantees time never goes backward. Perfect for duration measurement.
- Show the now_ns() helper from bench_main.cpp:
```cpp
static inline uint64_t now_ns() {
    struct timespec ts;
    clock_gettime(CLOCK_MONOTONIC, &ts);
    return static_cast<uint64_t>(ts.tv_sec) * 1'000'000'000ULL
         + static_cast<uint64_t>(ts.tv_nsec);
}
```
- CLOCK_MONOTONIC_RAW (Linux) avoids NTP frequency adjustments entirely — slightly more stable but not portable.
- Resolution of CLOCK_MONOTONIC is typically 1ns on modern Linux kernels with CONFIG_HZ_1000.

Add a summary section at the end listing all 7 key takeaways as bullet points.

The file should be completely self-contained HTML. No external JS or CSS dependencies. Write it now.

